  "step_3": "The decision reason evaluates lotteries based on the probability of loss (i.e., 0 Euros) and prefers the one with the lower loss probability. The individual acknowledges the similarity in probabilities and ultimately chooses based on having a 'higher' probability—specifically, the higher chance of a positive outcome (34% vs. 33%), not the lower chance of loss (66% vs. 67%). While the two perspectives (lower loss vs. higher gain probability) are numerically equivalent in this binary case, the individual frames their reasoning around maximizing the chance of winning, not minimizing the chance of losing. The decision reason emphasizes loss framing and minimization, whereas the individual's justification centers on gain probability maximization. There is no indication that the individual computed or compared loss probabilities directly. Therefore, although the numerical basis overlaps, the cognitive focus (gain vs. loss) differs. The reasoning process described does not align with the decision reason's rule as framed around loss avoidance.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
